 {"id":"DF-2026-3","title":"P1 \u2014 prod :3000 serves a 2026-10-06 build: the DF-1013 fix family (landed 10-14/15, commits c2b75c89..2038d9fb) is merged but NOT deployed \u2014 the fleet's memory backend runs pre-fix code","priority":"P1","status":"pending","created_by":"duckbrain-dogfood-run17b","created_at":"2026-10-16T14:10:00Z","updated_at":"2026-10-16T14:10:00Z","reasoning":"STALE-DEPLOY evidence (2026-10-16): the systemd --user unit duckbrain-http.service (PID 3222190, started Sat Oct 10 16:45:00) serves the prebuilt dist/ tree, whose newest code is Oct 6 10:41 (dist/src Oct 7 03:51) \u2014 every DF-1013 fix src file is NEWER than its dist counterpart. Proven by A/B against a HEAD scratch daemon: stale prod returns 400 VALIDATION_ERROR 'Unknown query parameter(s): debug' for ?debug=search while HEAD returns 200 with the documented searchDebug object (docs/api/http-api.md DF-1013-03 section promises the opposite); grep 'debug' in dist/src/http/routes/memories.js finds 0 hits. Also latent on prod: single-flight rebuilds (DF-1013-04), corrupt-sidecar union facets, anonymous-author writes (DF-1013-01), and S3-GIT-008 sha256 sync. The running argv also still carries --bind-all although the current unit file dropped it, so the process predates even the current unit definition. Fix direction: pnpm build:cli then systemctl --user restart duckbrain-http.service, then verify \u2014 (1) process start time is newer than dist mtime, (2) GET /health reports embedding.provider=openai, (3) an authenticated GET /api/memories?q=...&debug=search returns searchDebug (or 400 'debug=search requires q=' for the flag without q), (4) dist grep finds searchDebug. Do NOT treat the merged commits as delivered until the restart lands: every agent hitting :3000 (scheduler sync lanes, MCP-over-HTTP consumers) is on the old binary right now."}
 {"id":"DF-2026-4","title":"P2 \u2014 `duckbrain s3` resolves its namespace root from the process CWD instead of the config path every other verb resolves, and prints no resolved-root line, so it can silently sync a different tree","priority":"P2","status":"pending","created_by":"duckbrain-dogfood-run17b","created_at":"2026-10-16T14:10:00Z","updated_at":"2026-10-16T14:10:00Z","reasoning":"Reproduced on HEAD (a9350b94 + build 2026-10-16) with DUCKBRAIN_CONFIG_PATH pointing at a scratch root whose namespaces dir holds 3 files: `node bin/duckbrain.js s3 status <ns>` and `s3 sync <ns> push` run from the repo cwd report local=0 / 'Namespace not found' while a direct node probe of the same env walks 3 files; the same command run with cwd=<scratch root> works. Mechanism (src/s3/cli.ts): s3Command defaults configDir='.', and its local namespacesPath() resolves path.resolve(configDir, cfg.namespacesPath) \u2014 cwd-anchored \u2014 whereas list/search/namespace verbs resolve the root from DUCKBRAIN_CONFIG_PATH/DUCKBRAIN_HOME_ROOT (src/config/index.ts resolveDuckbrainRoot, which exists precisely because cwd-derived paths created stray trees before, per its own doc comment). Consequence seen live: with a leaked DUCKBRAIN_* env the CLI silently walked a stale /tmp tree (no error, walk=0), and cross-instance invocations collided on '.s3state/.lock' ('another sync is in progress') from a DIFFERENT root's lock. The README only documents the cwd rule obliquely ('A repo clone does not put duckbrain on PATH \u2014 run it via the repo's bin script'), which makes the repo cwd the accidental de-facto root. Fix direction: route s3 verbs through resolveDuckbrainRoot/resolveNamespacesPath like every other surface (or accept an explicit --config dir flag), and print the resolved namespaces root once per s3 command so a wrong-root invocation is visible in the first line of output instead of a silent empty walk."}
 {"id":"INT-CI-032","title":"P1 CI red on main at 6d0a0184: 'Run type check' fails with TS6133 \"'nsDir' is declared but its value is never read\" (run 39025187007)","priority":"P1","status":"pending","created_by":null,"created_at":"2026-10-17T13:15:18Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T13:15:18Z","reasoning":"Supervisor tick 2026-10-17: GitHub API jobs for run 39025187007 (wojons/duckbrain, public repo, real runner 1000016290) show job 'test' failure at step 'Run type check'; check-run annotation states \"'nsDir' is declared but its value is never read.\" (TS6133-class unused local) and 'Process completed with exit code 1.'. Head 6d0a0184 'fix(supa2): route PATCH/DELETE through NamespaceWriter'. Not billing (runner assigned, steps executed). Distinct from closed INT-CI-030/031. Fix direction: remove or use the unused nsDir binding, then npx tsc --noEmit locally before pushing.","capability_tags":["ci","typescript","types"],"complexity":1,"detail":{"fingerprint":"407afeb3f67231bee64b65e80c9a615ddc616c5dba77f089dcbf9f1a1663e7b5","evidence":[{"run_id":"39025187007","ts":"2026-10-17T13:15:18Z"}]}}
+{"id":"DB-SUPA-2-R1","title":"P2 DB-SUPA-2 residual (AC3): the 'concurrent cross-process PATCH writer' test is two SEQUENTIAL in-process PATCHes - no cross-process no-lost-updates proof exists","priority":"P2","status":"pending","created_by":null,"created_at":"2026-10-17T20:17:18Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T20:17:18Z","reasoning":"DB-SUPA-2 acceptance criterion 3 asks for \"a concurrent cross-process writer test proving no lost updates, plus a post-delete audit/SSE replay test\". The second half is covered (declared-table PATCH/DELETE audit-record tests plus the SSE CHANGELOG-not-CORRUPT test in src/http/routes/tables-supa2.test.ts). The first half is NOT: the describe block named \"concurrent cross-process PATCH writer\" contains exactly one test, \"two sequential PATCHes via same writer -> deterministic final state\", which issues two awaited PATCHes to one in-process Express app. Sequential in-process requests cannot lose an update, so the test cannot fail for the reason the criterion exists.\n\nWhy it is not a formality: the code path under test is a read-merge-rewrite (PATCH reads the row, merges the body, rewrites the JSONL file) guarded by the namespace lock file src/serialization/lock.ts. That is exactly the shape that loses updates when two OS processes interleave read and write, and lock.ts is also used by the S3 sync and autocommit paths on the same namespaces. Only a real second process can exercise cross-process lock acquisition, dead-pid recovery, stale-owner preemption and the nonce fence.\n\nRequired evidence (all in a scratch directory, no write to production namespaces): two or more separate OS processes (child_process spawned node/tsx, NOT in-process promises) reading and patching the same declared-table row in one namespace root; assert the final row is deterministic for last-writer-wins and that no acked update is missing from the resulting row history/audit ledger; and keep at least one assertion that fails if the namespace lock is removed. Report the observed interleaving (winner, timing) rather than only a green summary.\n\nFiled by the foreman tick duckbrain-foreman-2026-10-17-19-25-23 next to DB-SUPA-2 (commit 295e409d) so the residual is not lost; the same gap was disclosed in the DB-SUPA-2 close-out.","capability_tags":["typescript","testing","concurrency"],"complexity":3,"detail":{"fingerprint":"d6508f02c7f290d064701531f4939563728553b67b4aeda49237be7a29bf93ab"}}
